(*
Issue #551's own reproduction: a flat 'const x = 1+1+1+...+1;' chain (no
parens at all, so Parser::ExprDepth's recursive-call-based ceiling never
fires -- parseSimpleExpr's addop loop folds it ITERATIVELY, not through
nested calls) builds a BinaryExpr tree exactly 300,001 terms deep.
Sema's own checkExpr/ExprDepth (MaxExprDepth=1000, Sema.h) correctly
rejects the expression with a clean diagnostic -- but before this fix, the
process still segfaulted immediately afterward, tearing down the
already-fully-parsed, 300,001-deep AST: the compiler-generated,
implicitly recursive plang::BinaryExpr::~BinaryExpr() recursed one real
C++ stack frame per link in the chain. Fixed by giving BinaryExpr (and its
chain-shaped siblings -- see AstExprDestroy.cpp) a hand-written, iterative
destructor instead. Checked in verbatim rather than generated at build
time -- lit has no loop-emitting primitive, and the source is
deterministic; nobody should have to look at 300,001 '+1' terms to
read this file, so the lit directives come first.

A crash surfaces as a real signal (SIGSEGV etc.), not just a nonzero exit
code -- plain `not` (without --crash) already distinguishes the two, the
same way every sibling test in this directory does: this test's whole
point is that Sema's own clean rejection must not be followed by a raw
crash on the way out.
*)

(*
RUN: not %plang %s -o %t 2> %t.err
RUN: FileCheck %s < %t.err
*)

(*
CHECK: nested too deeply
*)

program p;
const x = ;
begin writeln(x); end.
